{"candidates":[{"claim":"Decentralized networks become correlated through algorithmic convergence on shared metric signals, so a large service with internally enforced architectural diversity is strictly more resilient to correlated failure.","mechanism":"If independent operators monitor the same external metrics (e.g., cloud provider status, market signals, or traffic thresholds) and use similar auto-scaling models, their independent decisions synchronize in phase. Even without communication, identical optimization criteria on identical inputs produce algorithmic convergence. The network appears uncorrelated during normal operation but fails in perfect synchrony when the shared signal crosses a threshold. A large service, by contrast, can internally enforce architectural diversity (different runtimes, redundancy protocols, and failure modes) that independent operators cannot coordinate on.","counterconditions":["Decentralized operators use heterogeneous, locally-tuned decision rules with diverse thresholds and models","Operators are causally firewalled from shared external signals by protocol design","The large service's internal diversity is cosmetic, masking a shared single point of failure"],"typicality":0.9,"analogy":{"source_memory_refs":["SRC_001","SRC_008"],"shared_structure":["Independent oscillators coupled through a shared external signal synchronize without direct communication","Coupling strength increases as the signal crosses thresholds, producing phase-locked catastrophic failure","The synchrony is invisible during normal operation and only manifests at the critical threshold"],"disanalogies":["Flock synchrony arises from real-time sensory feedback loops, while service synchrony arises from discrete threshold-triggered decisions","Birds have no shared optimization objective, while operators share an economic incentive to converge on best practices","Flocks have physical proximity constraints; decentralized services can be globally distributed"],"transfer_claims":["A network of independent operators coupled to the same market signal behaves as a single effective organism during failures","The leader the flock doesn't know it has is the shared market signal","Diversity that is not enforced by design will erode toward convergence under competitive pressure"],"overturn_conditions":["Empirical measurement shows decentralized operators maintain genuinely heterogeneous decision rules over time","Phase-locking requires direct communication and does not emerge from shared external signals alone","The large service's enforced diversity is itself a correlated failure mode (e.g., all diverse subsystems depend on the same internal orchestration layer)"]},"optional_refs":["SRC_001","SRC_002","SRC_008"],"sidecar":{"search_signal":"productive"}},{"claim":"The apparent resilience advantage of decentralized networks is an observer bias artifact: extreme value statistics ensure that the shared cause capable of synchronizing independent operators is discovered only after catastrophic failure, making independence unfalsifiable.","mechanism":"For any finite observation window, decentralized operators appear uncorrelated because the shared cause (e.g., a specific upstream provider failure, a TLS certificate expiry, or a DNS outage) has not yet occurred. When it does occur, the failure is retrospectively reclassified as correlated. This is an extreme value statistics problem: the rare shared cause exists from the start but is below detection threshold until it triggers. The claim 'independent until a shared cause is found' is unfalsifiable because the shared cause can always be found post hoc, making the superiority of decentralization an artifact of observation window rather than a structural property.","counterconditions":["We can identify, in advance, a complete enumeration of shared causes and observe that decentralized operators do not share any of them","The observation window is long enough to capture multiple rare shared-cause events and the network remains uncorrelated","The shared cause is genuinely novel and could not have been enumerated in advance, making the reclassification principled rather than post hoc"],"typicality":0.7,"optional_refs":["SRC_004","SRC_006"],"sidecar":{"search_signal":"productive"}},{"claim":"Resilience to correlated failure is determined by causal firebreaks in network topology, not by scale or operational independence: a decentralized network is more resilient only if its topology is organized to prevent cascade propagation.","mechanism":"Correlated failure propagates through shared dependencies (power grids, network backbones, common software libraries). A decentralized network reduces correlated failure risk only if its topology includes causal firebreaks—points where failure propagation is blocked. Scale and operational independence are neither necessary nor sufficient: a decentralized network with dense interdependencies can be more fragile than a large service with internal firebreaks, and a large service with poor internal isolation can be more fragile than a decentralized network with natural firebreaks. The distinguishing evidence is whether the topology contains verifiable causal firebreaks, not whether operators are nominally independent.","counterconditions":["The decentralized network's topology has no causal firebreaks and relies on shared infrastructure","The large service has no internal firebreaks and fails as a monolith","Causal firebreaks are necessary but not sufficient, and some other factor dominates resilience"],"typicality":0.85,"optional_refs":["SRC_005"],"sidecar":{"search_signal":"productive"}},{"claim":"A large service with strictly more total internal redundancy is more resilient because it can afford defense-in-depth and internally enforced architectural diversity, while decentralized operators converge on the same best practices under competitive pressure, eroding the diversity that would protect them.","mechanism":"Independent operators face competitive pressure to adopt best practices, leading to convergence on identical software stacks, cloud providers, and operational procedures. This convergence creates a correlated failure mode: all operators fail together when the shared best practice fails. A large service can enforce architectural diversity (multiple runtimes, redundant protocols, heterogeneous failure modes) that competitive independent operators cannot sustain because adopting a different practice is individually costly even if it is collectively beneficial. The large service's enforced diversity is a public good that the market cannot provide.","counterconditions":["Decentralized operators maintain diversity through regulatory mandates or protocol design","The large service's internal diversity is undermined by internal homogenization pressures","The cost of enforced diversity exceeds the expected benefit of reduced correlated failure"],"typicality":0.6,"optional_refs":["SRC_003"],"sidecar":{"search_signal":"productive"}},{"claim":"Independent operation does not imply independent failure: the distinguishing evidence is whether operators share a common dependency graph, and truly independent failures require provably disjoint dependency graphs, not merely separate management.","mechanism":"Two independently operated services can still share all their critical dependencies (same cloud region, same CDN, same TLS certificate authority). Their failures appear uncorrelated until the shared dependency fails, at which point they fail together. The causal mechanism for distinguishing truly independent failures from merely uncorrelated ones is the dependency graph: failures are truly independent only if the dependency graphs are provably disjoint (no shared power, network, software, or data dependencies). A large service with internally enforced diversity can have disjoint dependency graphs by design, while decentralized operators cannot guarantee this without coordination that undermines their independence.","counterconditions":["Decentralized operators can and do coordinate to ensure disjoint dependency graphs without losing operational independence","The large service cannot achieve truly disjoint internal dependency graphs due to cost or complexity constraints","Dependency graph overlap is not the primary driver of correlated failure; other mechanisms dominate"],"typicality":0.8,"optional_refs":["SRC_002","SRC_007"],"sidecar":{"search_signal":"productive"}},{"claim":"Resilience to correlated failure is not a function of network architecture but of whether shared causes are actively monitored and mitigated: the question conflates structural resilience with operational vigilance, and the evidence that distinguishes truly independent failures is the presence of active shared-cause detection, not topology.","mechanism":"Correlated failures are caused by shared causes (shared dependencies, shared environmental conditions, shared software). Whether these shared causes are discovered before or after the catastrophic failure depends on whether there is active monitoring for them. A decentralized network that does not monitor for shared causes will discover them post hoc (appearing uncorrelated until failure), while a large service that actively monitors for internal common-mode failures will catch them before they propagate. The distinguishing evidence is the presence and effectiveness of shared-cause monitoring, not the network topology. This reframes the debate: the question is not 'which architecture is more resilient' but 'which architecture enables better detection of shared causes.'","counterconditions":["Topology determines whether shared causes can propagate, regardless of monitoring","Active monitoring is equally feasible in both architectures and thus does not distinguish them","Shared-cause detection is ineffective because novel shared causes cannot be anticipated"],"typicality":0.5,"optional_refs":["SRC_006"],"sidecar":{"search_signal":"productive"}}],"scratch_proposal":{"new_blocks":[{"local_key":"NEW_001","body":{"content":"What if we model decentralized operators as coupled oscillators where the coupling is the shared market signal? The Kuramoto model predicts that above a critical coupling strength, oscillators synchronize spontaneously. If we operationalize 'coupling strength' as the similarity of operators' decision rules (thresholds, models, data), we can predict when a decentralized network will phase-lock and fail in synchrony. This would provide a quantitative mechanism for the algorithmic convergence hypothesis.","possible_next_move":"Design a simulation where N independent operators each decide whether to reboot/scale based on a shared signal plus noise. Vary the noise level and measure the synchronization of failures. The hypothesis is that below a critical noise-to-signal ratio, the network fails in perfect synchrony despite no direct communication.","why_keep_this":"This connects SCR_002's market-synchronization idea to a well-studied mathematical framework (Kuramoto), potentially yielding a testable quantitative prediction.","bears_on_refs":["SRC_001","SRC_003"]}}],"links":[{"from_ref":"SCR_003","to_ref":"NEW_001","relation_hint":"The Kuramoto model provides a mathematical framework for SCR_003's question about whether leaderless protocols can prevent synchronization: the answer depends on whether the coupling strength (shared signal influence) can be kept below the critical threshold.","holds_when":"The Kuramoto model is a valid approximation of operator decision dynamics","weakens_when":"Operator decisions are not well-modeled by phase oscillators (e.g., they are discrete and stateful rather than continuous)"}],"unresolved_questions":[{"question":"If all operators use identical models for auto-scaling, is the resulting algorithmic convergence a shared cause in the same sense as a shared dependency? Or is it a different kind of shared cause that requires a different mitigation strategy?","related_refs":["SCR_004","NEW_001"]}]}}